---
// src/pages/security/index.astro
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Security, Privacy, and Trust | Grassroots Movement">
  <style is:global>
    .security-prose h2 {
      margin-top: 2.5rem;
      padding-top: 1.5rem;
      border-top: 1px solid #d8d2c6;
    }
    .security-prose h3 {
      margin-top: 1.75rem;
    }
    .security-prose h4 {
      margin-top: 1.25rem;
    }
    .security-prose p {
      margin-top: 0.75rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
    }
    .security-prose .info-list {
      margin-top: 0.75rem;
    }
    .security-prose h2:first-of-type {
      margin-top: 1.5rem;
      padding-top: 0;
      border-top: none;
    }
    /* Visible link styling - charcoal text, underline offset for readability */
    .security-prose a:not(.button) {
      color: #2b2b2b;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-decoration-color: #2b2b2b;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .security-prose a:not(.button):hover,
    .security-prose a:not(.button):focus {
      color: #8a3d23;
      text-decoration-color: #8a3d23;
    }
    /* Button links use brand bone text for legibility on dark background */
    .security-prose a.button {
      color: #f1ece1;
    }
    .security-prose a.button:hover,
    .security-prose a.button:focus {
      color: #ffffff;
    }
  </style>
  <main class="security-prose">
    <h1>Security, Privacy, and Trust</h1>
    <p>
      We built GrassrootsMVT to gather civic input without letting spam, bots, or coordinated
      manipulation drown out real voices. We also protect privacy by collecting only what we need
      to run the platform and publish results with integrity.
    </p>
    <p>This page explains:</p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li><a href="#reporting-tiers">How results are displayed by tier</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-we-store">How accounts and sessions work</a></li>
      <li><a href="#survey-integrity">How surveys are published, versioned, and counted</a></li>
      <li><a href="#verification">How location and address verification works, and what we store and do not store</a></li>
      <li><a href="#anti-spam">How we protect against spam and manipulation</a></li>
      <li><a href="#audit-trail">How we audit the system</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="reporting-tiers">Our reporting tiers</h2>
    <p>
      We publish results in tiers so the public can see the difference between broad participation
      and higher-confidence participation.
    </p>

    <h3>Tier 1: General Public, no verified address</h3>
    <p>This tier includes responses from all accounts, including those that have not completed address verification.</p>

    <h3>Tier 2: Registered Voter, verified address</h3>
    <p>This tier includes responses from accounts that completed address verification.</p>
    <p>From this tier, we offer filters so results can be viewed at different jurisdiction levels:</p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>State</li>
      <li>Federal House District</li>
      <li>State House District</li>
      <li>State Senate District</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We display these filters as a dropdown so the same verified dataset can be viewed at any
      jurisdiction level. Our plan is to scale down to wards within cities and counties as that
      data becomes available.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-store">What we store</h2>

    <h3>Account basics</h3>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>Your email address.</li>
      <li>A password hash, never the password itself.</li>
      <li>Session records to keep you logged in across page loads.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Session timeouts</h3>
    <p>We use two timeouts to keep accounts secure:</p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>An idle timeout that ends a session after a period of inactivity.</li>
      <li>An absolute timeout that ends a session after a fixed number of days.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="survey-integrity">Survey integrity</h2>
    <p>We record the following for every survey response:</p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>The exact survey definition used (SurveyJS JSON).</li>
      <li>A version hash so any wording change is visible and auditable.</li>
      <li>The response record with a timestamp.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Response editing without duplicate counting</h3>
    <p>Each account has one counted response per survey version. Editing works as follows:</p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>Users can revise answers at any time before a survey sunsets.</li>
      <li>A revision updates the existing response and creates an audit record.</li>
      <li>The survey list shows your completion date and edit status.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Survey transparency and versioning</h3>
    <p>We treat survey wording as a public artifact:</p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>Surveys are published from a JSONC source file.</li>
      <li>Each version receives a hash.</li>
      <li>Any change to wording creates a new version with a new hash.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This means anyone can compare versions and see exactly what changed.</p>

    <h2 id="verification">Verification, plain language</h2>
    <p>Verification helps us reduce manipulation without turning the platform into a surveillance system.</p>

    <h3>Address verification</h3>
    <p>For verified-address tiering, we focus on three things:</p>
    <ol class="info-list">
      <li>The address used to derive your districts.</li>
      <li>The jurisdiction keys derived from that address (state, house district, senate district).</li>
      <li>A verification outcome stored as a status plus audit events.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>What we do not store</h3>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>Plaintext passwords. We store a password hash, never the password itself.</li>
      <li>Ongoing device location. We do not use GPS or browser location as a live tracker.</li>
      <li>Live movement history. We do not build a profile of where your phone or browser goes over time.</li>
      <li>Personal identifiers in published results. Public result views are aggregated, not published as individual voter records.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Address coordinates and device location</h3>
    <p>
      Address coordinates and device location are different.
      Address coordinates describe a place (the address on file).
      Device location describes where a phone or browser is at a moment in time.
    </p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>We may store address coordinates to support district assignment and proximity checks.</li>
      <li>Address coordinates are address metadata, not a live movement trail.</li>
      <li>We do not store ongoing device location.</li>
      <li>If we run a home-of-record proximity check, we store only the result context: pass/fail, distance, accuracy, and timestamp.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Wyoming</h3>
    <p>
      Wyoming provides candidates access to a voter file. For Wyoming accounts, we use that voter
      file to confirm whether an account matches a registered voter and to support district-level
      reporting.
    </p>
    <p>
      Coordinates, when stored, are tied to the registered address and district verification process.
      They are not used to follow a person or create a live movement profile.
    </p>
    <p>
      Published results are aggregated. We do not publish individual names, email addresses, voter
      identifiers, addresses, or personal location records in survey result views. We use voter file
      data provided by your elected Secretary of State.
    </p>
    <p>
      Candidates wishing to participate may contact Jimmy Skovgard via
      <a href="https://skovgard2026.org" rel="external">skovgard2026.org</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>National</h3>
    <p>
      For broader participation across the country, we use an address-based jurisdiction method.
      We ask for an address, derive districts, and store the jurisdiction keys needed for aggregation.
      Internal deduplication and consistency checks may use signals such as an address hash.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not store ongoing device location. If we add or use a proximity check, we ask the user
      to confirm location for the home-of-record process, not to track movement over time.
    </p>

    <h2 id="anti-spam">Anti-spam protections</h2>
    <p>We use multiple layers:</p>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>A bot challenge on sensitive actions, including signup and other high-risk endpoints.</li>
      <li>Rate limits on abuse-prone endpoints.</li>
      <li>One counted response per user per survey version, unless the survey design explicitly allows updates.</li>
      <li>Audit events that flag suspicious patterns for review.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="audit-trail">Audit trail</h2>
    <p>
      We record security-relevant events so we can investigate problems and show a clear history
      of what happened. This includes login activity, verification attempts, response submissions,
      and administrative actions.
    </p>

    <h2>Your control and consent</h2>
    <ul class="info-list">
      <li>Participation is always optional.</li>
      <li>Address verification is optional, but unlocks district-level result views.</li>
      <li>Device-location checks are not used as ongoing tracking or movement history.</li>
      <li>You can sign in at any time to revise your survey answers, unless the survey has closed. Most surveys allow revisions.</li>
      <li>In the future, we plan to offer mail-based account verification, using a one-time code sent to your physical address, as an additional layer of identity assurance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Feedback</h2>
    <p>
      Security is not a static promise. It is a process. We invite feedback and we publish the
      results.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/surveys/security-feedback" data-auth-nav="/surveys/security-feedback">Take the security feedback survey</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
